  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 227 AM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: 500 mb flow has gone mostly zonal promoting\nweaker variety systems rather than deeply forced events this\nweek. The next front arrives late this afternoon and will move\nthrough this evening. Upper level support remains anemic with\nprimary forcing existing in the 850-700mb layer. Timing coupled\nwith forcing will limit the threat of stronger storms, but CAPE\naround 1000j/kg and moderate shear could support a rarer \nstronger cell if it can efficiently tap into low level \ninstability or if outflow interacts with the surface front. \nPWATs climbing above 1.5\" will favor some heavy shower elements \nand a few cells could produce localized heavy rainfall resulting\nin ponding. As noted above, flow will be quite progressive \nleading to a fast exit tonight with drier conditions for \nTuesday. Northwest flow tonight increases, especially across\nLake Champlain, and may result in some rough lake conditions. \nDry weather lingers through Wednesday as the Bermuda High \ncontinues its slow retreat into the Atlantic. Temperatures will \nslowly trend cooler with highs remaining in the mid/upper 70s to\nlow 80s across northern New York and Vermont. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A shortwave trough looks to push \nthrough the region Thursday into Friday, bringing widespread \nshower chances towards the end of the week. Deterministic \nguidance continues to be in good agreement on the general \npattern evolution, though there is still some uncertainty \nregarding the timing of the system. The current forecast shows \nrain chances ramping up quickly during the early morning hours \non Thursday with widespread rainfall expected across the upper \nlevel trough swings through Thursday afternoon. As we get closer\nwe will have to monitor the potential for a few stronger \nthunderstorms during the afternoon on Thursday, with instability\nthe biggest question at this time. Behind the trough, a cold \nfront is expected to bring below normal temperatures and drier \nweather to the region for Friday into the upcoming weekend. Deep\nlayer ridging is expected to develop by Saturday which should \nbring plenty of sunshine with highs in the 70s.",
